## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical log parsing under rotation, mixed formats, gz archives, and multi-line blocks. It also checks ability to design robust pipelines with standard UNIX tools.

## Key Concepts
- POSIX text processing (awk, sed)
- gzipped logs via zcat
- JSON vs text parsing
- multi-line block handling
- time bucketing by hour

## Code Example
```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
cutoff=$(date -d '6 hours ago' '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
zcat /logs/app/**/*.log.gz /logs/app/**/*.log 2>/dev/null | awk -v c="$cutoff" '
  function hour(ts){ return substr(ts,1,13) }
  {
    if($0 ~ /"ts":/) { match($0, /"ts":"([^\"]+)"/, m); ts=m[1]; match($0, /"src":"([^\"]+)"/, s); ip=s[1] }
    else if($0 ~ /^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/) { ts=$1" "$2 }
    if(ts && ip && /FAILED/){ h=hour(ts); buckets[h][ip]=1 }
  }
  END{ for (h in buckets) print h, length(buckets[h]) }
'
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to include a sliding 6-hour window?
- How would you parallelize across many hosts and aggregate results?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to unify heterogeneous log formats, handle compressed data, and perform time-based validation in a tight, production-like scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-format timestamp extraction (text and JSON)
- Decompression with zcat for .gz files
- Epoch conversion via date -d
- Sliding window filtering (60 minutes) and max absolute skew

## Code Example
```bash
find /logs -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) -print0 | while IFS= read -d '' f; do
  case "$f" in
    *.gz) data=$(zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo) ;;
    *) data=$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo) ;;
  esac
  echo "$data" | awk '
    /^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
      cmd = "date -d \"" $1 " " $2 "\" +%s 2>/dev/null"
      cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
      if (ts > 0) print ts
    }
    /{.*"ts".*"lvl".*"msg".*}/ {
      match($0, /"ts":[[:space:]]*[0-9]+/)
      if (RSTART > 0) {
        ts = substr($0, RSTART + 5, RLENGTH - 5)
        print ts
      }
    }
  '
done | awk -v now="$(date +%s)" '
  {
    diff = now - $1
    if (diff <= 3600 && diff >= -3600) {
      abs_diff = (diff < 0) ? -diff : diff
      if (abs_diff > max) max = abs_diff
    }
  }
  END { print max }
'
```
